1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,591
Most people will tell you this is a
wedding portrait of a pregnant bride.

2
00:00:05,701 --> 00:00:09,199
Look closer, because
it is probably neither.

3
00:00:12,179 --> 00:00:16,648
Her green gown was a stock fertility
pose, not a sign of pregnancy.

4
00:00:16,758 --> 00:00:21,675
The fur lining is a boast, a
billboard for Bruges luxury.

5
00:00:21,785 --> 00:00:24,831
The dog at her feet
echoes medieval tombs,

6
00:00:24,851 --> 00:00:29,694
where dogs lay at a dead woman's
feet to guide her into the afterlife.

7
00:00:29,804 --> 00:00:34,553
And Costanza Trenta, the likely
sitter, had died in 1433,

8
00:00:34,813 --> 00:00:37,039
a year before the
painting was finished.

9
00:00:37,369 --> 00:00:40,567
The convex mirror holds two
visitors in the doorway,

10
00:00:40,846 --> 00:00:42,698
perhaps the painter himself.

11
00:00:42,808 --> 00:00:47,352
Ten tiny roundels of Christ's
Passion circle the glass.

12
00:00:47,462 --> 00:00:52,080
Every scene after Christ's
death sits on Costanza's side.

13
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:55,680
Compare it with Van Eyck's
Canon van der Paele altarpiece,

14
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:57,590
painted the same year.

15
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:02,318
The same mastery of rugs and
rich cloth runs through both.

16
00:01:02,428 --> 00:01:05,963
But the clue that matters
most is in the hands.

17
00:01:06,073 --> 00:01:10,878
Giovanni holds hers so lightly, as
if she were already slipping away.

18
00:01:11,208 --> 00:01:14,294
Pull back, and the
whole picture changes.

19
00:01:14,404 --> 00:01:19,770
It reads less like a wedding record than
a memorial to a love that outlasts death.

